467
00:32:28,880 --> 00:32:30,700
 You compute the hash proof of stake.

468
00:32:30,700 --> 00:32:35,520
 You prove that you have some number of the token to bid on a slot.

469
00:32:35,520 --> 00:32:40,360
 Proof of authority was just like, well, there are these like people that we trust and each

470
00:32:40,360 --> 00:32:42,520
 one of them runs a node, right?

471
00:32:42,520 --> 00:32:47,200
 And like we just like, we know who they are, we've seen their passports, something like

472
00:32:47,200 --> 00:32:48,200
 that.

473
00:32:48,200 --> 00:32:50,640
 And it sounds a lot like that.

474
00:32:50,640 --> 00:32:54,200
 Back in the day when there was a lot of bridging going on, there were a lot of proof of authority

475
00:32:54,200 --> 00:32:55,200
 bridges.

476
00:32:55,200 --> 00:32:58,960
 I'm thinking of Ronan and wormhole as two examples.

477
00:32:58,960 --> 00:33:02,960
 Wormhole famously was like, you know, there was this joke that it was a multisig between

478
00:33:02,960 --> 00:33:05,040
 friends.

479
00:33:05,040 --> 00:33:11,800
 Because it was, it was literally just wormhole, like the jump trading like bridge.

480
00:33:11,800 --> 00:33:12,800
 Correct.

481
00:33:12,800 --> 00:33:17,920
 It's just like the come, come get scalped by these professional traders and use our bridge

482
00:33:17,920 --> 00:33:19,240
 bridge.

483
00:33:19,240 --> 00:33:23,520
 Come get scalped until it gets obliterated for 300 million by Lazarus.

484
00:33:23,520 --> 00:33:28,040
 And then it must have been making so much money that they just filled that hole.

485
00:33:28,040 --> 00:33:34,360
 So, so yeah, I mean, let me go use this bridge that's like, you know, operated by this swarm

486
00:33:34,360 --> 00:33:40,080
 of sharks and like completely undecentralized by the way.

487
00:33:40,080 --> 00:33:42,960
 So like, super cool.

488
00:33:42,960 --> 00:33:47,200
 Yeah, I think it reminds me a little bit of that.

489
00:33:47,200 --> 00:33:49,960
 Typically when, when protocols would say that we're proof of authority, that would have

490
00:33:49,960 --> 00:33:55,080
 these elaborate plans to decentralize, which never happened, of course.

491
00:33:55,080 --> 00:33:58,280
 But it sounds like this is the network operating as expected.

492
00:33:58,280 --> 00:34:01,600
 So something to think about.

493
00:34:01,600 --> 00:34:04,680
 Yeah.

494
00:34:04,680 --> 00:34:09,800
 I think two other things I wanted to cover with Spark.

495
00:34:09,800 --> 00:34:16,680
 One is the story around unilateral exit, which is possible when they first launched.

496
00:34:16,680 --> 00:34:24,200
 I don't think that they had actually like built like a software tool for you to do this.

497
00:34:24,200 --> 00:34:28,200
 And people kind of made some noise about it and then they subsequently did.

498
00:34:28,200 --> 00:34:34,400
 And the thing about the unilateral exit is like the most commonly Spark is being pitched

499
00:34:34,400 --> 00:34:39,840
 these days is like, put your spending wallet on it or like use it to onboard millions of

500
00:34:39,840 --> 00:34:41,680
 users in the developing world.

501
00:34:41,680 --> 00:34:45,960
 And so that means they'll probably have like relatively small balances.

502
00:34:45,960 --> 00:34:51,580
 And then also when you transact a lot on Spark, your Bitcoin tends to get broken up into like

503
00:34:51,580 --> 00:34:56,680
 multiple leaves, what they call them, but you can think about them as roughly similar

504
00:34:56,680 --> 00:34:59,760
 UTXOs on layer one.

505
00:34:59,760 --> 00:35:05,800
 But so you have like a lot of small valued leaves of Spark and each of those leaves needs

506
00:35:05,800 --> 00:35:12,600
 to be unilaterally exit and the cost basically like kind of explodes the more leaves you have

507
00:35:12,600 --> 00:35:15,520
 and also the deeper the leaves get.

508
00:35:15,520 --> 00:35:22,000
 And so you can have a wallet where it costs like a very significant portion of the wallet

509
00:35:22,000 --> 00:35:25,440
 and transaction fees in order to actually unilaterally exit.

510
00:35:25,440 --> 00:35:33,480
 So this guy, Open Noms, who is kind of like a legend hacker in the Bitcoin space, he builds

511
00:35:33,480 --> 00:35:35,680
 like Raspy Blitz and stuff like that.

512
00:35:35,680 --> 00:35:38,360
 And he works for Blink, which is like a lightning wallet.

513
00:35:38,360 --> 00:35:44,000
 He ran this test on Spark where he tried to withdraw, he had a hundred thousand Satoshi

514
00:35:44,000 --> 00:35:45,000
 wallet.

515
00:35:45,000 --> 00:35:50,120
 So that's like about a hundred dollars at the time, I think maybe a little bit less.

516
00:35:50,120 --> 00:35:52,880
 And he tried to use this unilateral exit thing.

517
00:35:52,880 --> 00:36:02,400
 And his wallet had 22 leaves and it required 253 transaction packages to exit and it cost

518
00:36:02,400 --> 00:36:08,920
 him something like 80% of the wallet value and on-chain fees to recover the other 20%.

519
00:36:08,920 --> 00:36:14,000
 So you had to spend 80,000 Satoshis to recover 20,000 Satoshis out of the Spark and in a

520
00:36:14,000 --> 00:36:15,600
 unilateral exit.

521
00:36:15,600 --> 00:36:22,400
 So that's basically uneconomical in terms of the story for unilateral exit for small amounts.

522
00:36:22,400 --> 00:36:24,800
 So that's pretty bad.

523
00:36:24,800 --> 00:36:29,800
 Another thing there is that in order to do this unilateral exit, you need this recovery

524
00:36:29,800 --> 00:36:34,200
 backup that basically backs up the state of your wallet.

525
00:36:34,200 --> 00:36:39,680
 And as far as I know, no consumer wallets that are doing Spark make this available to

526
00:36:39,680 --> 00:36:44,360
 the user or do something like iCloud backup where it is being made available.

527
00:36:44,360 --> 00:36:49,360
 And so if you don't have this data, when you suddenly need to unilateral exit, you're shy

528
00:36:49,360 --> 00:36:51,280
 out of luck.

529
00:36:51,280 --> 00:36:55,640
 So I think that's something that might be a little bit overlooked in the ecosystem.

530
00:36:55,640 --> 00:36:57,600
 So that's the story about unilateral exit.

531
00:36:57,600 --> 00:37:01,640
 And then the last thing is the privacy story.

532
00:37:01,640 --> 00:37:04,880
 Spark is not private.

533
00:37:04,880 --> 00:37:06,500
 As far as I understand, it could be.

534
00:37:06,500 --> 00:37:11,080
 I think the technology to make this kind of construct private from the operators exists,

535
00:37:11,080 --> 00:37:14,920
 but they, I think, have made a choice not to make it private from the operators.

536
00:37:14,920 --> 00:37:19,400
 So when you transact on Spark, all of your transaction activity is viewable and attributable

537
00:37:19,400 --> 00:37:24,120
 to you by the Spark entity.

538
00:37:24,120 --> 00:37:29,120
 So just know that, that it's basically a custodial privacy kind of situation.

539
00:37:29,120 --> 00:37:35,960
 And in the past, it was even worse than that, where I think that they had an account model.

540
00:37:35,960 --> 00:37:40,600
 So you weren't creating a new address every time you received Spark.

541
00:37:40,600 --> 00:37:43,720
 You were receiving and sending out of the same address constantly.

542
00:37:43,720 --> 00:37:47,520
 So that means that if somebody had your address, they could see all your transaction activity.

543
00:37:47,520 --> 00:37:53,880
 And by default, all of the Spark transaction activity was published on a public block explorer

544
00:37:53,880 --> 00:37:54,880
 for funsies.

545
00:37:54,880 --> 00:38:03,600
 There was no reason to do this other than I don't really understand why.

546
00:38:03,600 --> 00:38:08,400
 I think they were trying to go for a token trading speculation use case.

547
00:38:08,400 --> 00:38:14,840
 And so they were trying to pattern match the Ethereum Solana world, where that's more the

548
00:38:14,840 --> 00:38:15,840
 case.

549
00:38:15,840 --> 00:38:21,400
 But yeah, so basically, all of your transaction activity was-- and it wasn't like it was published

550
00:38:21,400 --> 00:38:22,400
 on some blockchain.

551
00:38:22,400 --> 00:38:25,360
 And that's why there's a block explorer, because it needs to be public.

552
00:38:25,360 --> 00:38:29,520
 It was like, this is a private ledger that has no reason to be public, but then they're

553
00:38:29,520 --> 00:38:34,920
 publishing it for the sake of transparency, which is kind of a head scratcher.

554
00:38:34,920 --> 00:38:40,480
 I believe now that you can set a flag in your Spark wallet that opts you out of that public

555
00:38:40,480 --> 00:38:42,960
 sharing.

556
00:38:42,960 --> 00:38:50,240
 But yeah, I don't think by default, Spark has very good privacy from the public.

557
00:38:50,240 --> 00:38:54,960
 And it doesn't have privacy at all from the Spark entity itself.

558
00:38:54,960 --> 00:38:55,960
 Wow.

559
00:38:55,960 --> 00:39:00,980
 I mean, poor Spark just got torn apart.

560
00:39:00,980 --> 00:39:06,920
 I mean, in terms of publicly publishing something, it's like, OK, it's not public, even if they

561
00:39:06,920 --> 00:39:08,760
 don't publish it, but it's still not private, right?

562
00:39:08,760 --> 00:39:11,480
 The Spark operators can see all of your transactions.

563
00:39:11,480 --> 00:39:20,680
 And if subpoenaed or if coerced somehow, they would be able to turn those over to an authority.

564
00:39:20,680 --> 00:39:21,680
 Or anyone, right?

565
00:39:21,680 --> 00:39:26,040
 I think that's the fear.

566
00:39:26,040 --> 00:39:27,360
 I want to say one thing.

567
00:39:27,360 --> 00:39:31,520
 There is a canonical way to decentralize these types of networks.

568
00:39:31,520 --> 00:39:36,360
 And I have to talk about it, because I've done it.

569
00:39:36,360 --> 00:39:43,800
 So the typical way to serve one of these L2s or out-of-bounds networks, like maybe you're

570
00:39:43,800 --> 00:39:50,280
 serving an exchange out of a threshold SIG or something like that, is you can do some

571
00:39:50,280 --> 00:39:56,120
 kind of bootleg version of proof of stake, where the validators, in this case, would

572
00:39:56,120 --> 00:39:59,960
 be the Spark operators are talking to each other and following a series of rules and

573
00:39:59,960 --> 00:40:06,480
 committing every increment of the rules that they're allowed to act upon to a shared blockchain.

574
00:40:06,480 --> 00:40:12,200
 And when one of them goes out of line, the others all vote to slash, which is to say

575
00:40:12,200 --> 00:40:14,920
 the others all vote to take away some of their stake, right?

576
00:40:14,920 --> 00:40:18,920
 So basically, punishing malfeasance for validators.

577
00:40:18,920 --> 00:40:24,280
 Does that work if the validators are all homies and business together?

578
00:40:24,280 --> 00:40:25,280
 It does not.

579
00:40:25,280 --> 00:40:26,280
 Ask me how I do it.

580
00:40:26,280 --> 00:40:28,940
 It's private permission validators, that.

581
00:40:28,940 --> 00:40:29,940
 It does not.

582
00:40:29,940 --> 00:40:31,280
 It does not work.

583
00:40:31,280 --> 00:40:39,400
 You need at least two, let's say 30-30-30 is the smallest ideal ratio, or what is called

584
00:40:39,400 --> 00:40:45,760
 the Nakamoto coefficient, which is how many actual individual entities are validating the

585
00:40:45,760 --> 00:40:51,160
 network, not how many different nodes, how many individual entities.

586
00:40:51,160 --> 00:40:55,680
 So that's the important number for these proof of stake.

587
00:40:55,680 --> 00:40:59,800
 Progressive decentralized, obviously not fully decentralized, but progressive decentralization

588
00:40:59,800 --> 00:41:00,800
 schemes.

589
00:41:00,800 --> 00:41:01,800
 Yeah.

590
00:41:01,800 --> 00:41:02,800
 Yeah.

591
00:41:02,800 --> 00:41:03,800
 Totally.

592
00:41:03,800 --> 00:41:09,120
 It makes a lot of sense.

593
00:41:09,120 --> 00:41:15,240
 And I think that this was conceived of maybe before there were a lot of breakthroughs on

594
00:41:15,240 --> 00:41:16,240
 the arc side.

595
00:41:16,240 --> 00:41:24,200
 And I think now a protocol like Arc looks like it has a lot of the benefits of Spark, but

596
00:41:24,200 --> 00:41:30,280
 with I think a much better trust model, like a unilaterally better trust model.

597
00:41:30,280 --> 00:41:32,860
 And that's even without covenants.

598
00:41:32,860 --> 00:41:38,580
 And if there ends up being a covenant soft fork that then enables the better forms of

599
00:41:38,580 --> 00:41:44,240
 Arc that people want to see, then I think that those are strictly superior.

600
00:41:44,240 --> 00:41:51,120
 So I'll be interested to see if Spark survives in a world like that, or maybe if they get

601
00:41:51,120 --> 00:41:57,320
 enough momentum and enough activity in their Spark and they don't feel any drive to upgrade

602
00:41:57,320 --> 00:42:03,960
 it to something like that, or maybe they don't agree that Arc is unilaterally better.

603
00:42:03,960 --> 00:42:09,200
 I do think it's worth maybe saying a few nice things about Spark.

604
00:42:09,200 --> 00:42:14,880
 So that was a little bit of a tear down, but yeah, I think the main things that are really

605
00:42:14,880 --> 00:42:21,000
 nice about Spark is I think that they have an amazing SDK and developer experience where

606
00:42:21,000 --> 00:42:26,920
 I think it's probably the easiest Bitcoin, L2, Bitcoin transaction thing to build into

607
00:42:26,920 --> 00:42:30,520
 any app and to build a wallet around.

608
00:42:30,520 --> 00:42:34,200
 It's not a super high bar because I think that the other ones like Lightning Network

609
00:42:34,200 --> 00:42:39,360
 are kind of infamously complex, but yeah, I think Spark has made that probably as smooth

610
00:42:39,360 --> 00:42:42,400
 as it could possibly be.

611
00:42:42,400 --> 00:42:52,080
 I think that it enables people to transact with Bitcoin very cheaply and very fast.

612
00:42:52,080 --> 00:42:58,440
 And so it's like getting more people using Bitcoin hopefully, and trying to promote transactional

613
00:42:58,440 --> 00:42:59,440
 use cases for Bitcoin.

614
00:42:59,440 --> 00:43:02,440
 Is there anything else you guys want to touch upon?

615
00:43:02,440 --> 00:43:05,840
 Yeah, also plugging into the existing rails, man.

616
00:43:05,840 --> 00:43:10,240
 So one issue with Lightning has always been that you have to have your Lightning node

617
00:43:10,240 --> 00:43:13,040
 online for it to be fully self custodial, right?

618
00:43:13,040 --> 00:43:18,000
 Otherwise, you're going through a Lightning provider in some configuration.

619
00:43:18,000 --> 00:43:23,400
 I think Spark unlocks out for a lot of people because of their federated setup that you

620
00:43:23,400 --> 00:43:26,720
 can rail out on Lightning, your Bitcoin super fast.

621
00:43:26,720 --> 00:43:28,560
 I think that's cool.

622
00:43:28,560 --> 00:43:32,520
 Well, shout out to LightSpark.

623
00:43:32,520 --> 00:43:35,280
 We don't hate Spark in entirety.

624
00:43:35,280 --> 00:43:42,080
 We just pointed out many of its potential flaws and that Arc is going to L2Mog Spark

625
00:43:42,080 --> 00:43:45,080
 in the short term.

626
00:43:45,080 --> 00:43:49,120
 Thank you, Tyler, for teaching us about Spark.

627
00:43:49,120 --> 00:43:58,480
 So much more information than I learned from reading the Spark blog comparing Bitcoin L2s.

628
00:43:58,480 --> 00:43:59,480
 Moving on.

629
00:43:59,480 --> 00:44:03,760
 All right, that's right.

630
00:44:03,760 --> 00:44:06,920
 Another topic, the slop auction.

631
00:44:06,920 --> 00:44:14,440
 Our very own Chris Doran wrote another beautiful blog talking about the slop that's polluting

632
00:44:14,440 --> 00:44:17,520
 our feeds and filling up the internet.

633
00:44:17,520 --> 00:44:24,640
 Chris, tell us, how do we end this auction and stop the slop?

634
00:44:24,640 --> 00:44:31,640
 All right, so there's this famous thing in MEV, Maximum Extractable Value.

635
00:44:31,640 --> 00:44:38,120
 There's a famous post by the kings of MEV called Flashbots, where basically what they

636
00:44:38,120 --> 00:44:44,760
 said is this, so blockchains have this scalability limit problem, right?

637
00:44:44,760 --> 00:44:45,800
 Everybody wants to transact there.

638
00:44:45,800 --> 00:44:50,840
 How do you decide who gets to transact and then how do you make sure that there is enough

639
00:44:50,840 --> 00:44:57,160
 space in each block in the blockchain to service all of the genuine transactions that people

640
00:44:57,160 --> 00:44:59,080
 will want to do?

641
00:44:59,080 --> 00:45:05,920
 Basically Flashbots went across a bunch of L2s and they were like, let's look at what

642
00:45:05,920 --> 00:45:10,000
 the actual transactions on these L2s are composed of.

643
00:45:10,000 --> 00:45:14,640
 And overwhelmingly, it's not genuine transactions.

644
00:45:14,640 --> 00:45:19,480
 The transactions that these L2s are mostly servicing are MEV transactions.

645
00:45:19,480 --> 00:45:24,320
 So when you're on chain, how is a MEV transaction not a real transaction?

646
00:45:24,320 --> 00:45:30,240
 Doesn't there have to be some associated economic activity for it to like vampire in order for

647
00:45:30,240 --> 00:45:36,000
 there to be MEV to extract and then like, yeah, correct.

648
00:45:36,000 --> 00:45:37,640
 But what is the ratio, right?

649
00:45:37,640 --> 00:45:44,200
 What is the ratio between me wanting to buy a dog poop coin and the 17 guys placing fizzling

650
00:45:44,200 --> 00:45:48,880
 orders to like try and sandwich me for 1% of my swap, right?

651
00:45:48,880 --> 00:45:50,120
 That's the question.

652
00:45:50,120 --> 00:45:52,120
 And it's not flattering.

653
00:45:52,120 --> 00:45:55,120
 It's not one-to-one.

654
00:45:55,120 --> 00:45:58,280
 I mean, that would be a beautiful world if it was.

655
00:45:58,280 --> 00:46:04,000
 So there's a personalized experience with my MEV bot, you know?

656
00:46:04,000 --> 00:46:08,640
 Yeah, like it's not just like you and your counterparty in the trade.

657
00:46:08,640 --> 00:46:12,560
 It's you, your counterparty and like 17 other people trying to like get a tiny slice of

658
00:46:12,560 --> 00:46:13,560
 it.

659
00:46:13,560 --> 00:46:14,560
 How do they all get a piece?

660
00:46:14,560 --> 00:46:20,640
 Wouldn't there just be one, like, one MEV bot that recognizes the entire opportunity

661
00:46:20,640 --> 00:46:25,800
 and builds the whole stack and then pays the biggest bribe to flash bots?

662
00:46:25,800 --> 00:46:26,800
 Right.

663
00:46:26,800 --> 00:46:28,560
 So here's kind of the crux of it.

664
00:46:28,560 --> 00:46:33,380
 You can, because you can create these very complicated on-chain orders using smart contracts,

665
00:46:33,380 --> 00:46:39,560
 you can just like dumbly execute them over and over again, like hoping to get something,

666
00:46:39,560 --> 00:46:40,560
 right?

667
00:46:40,560 --> 00:46:42,360
 And the worst case that happens is the transaction fizzles.

668
00:46:42,360 --> 00:46:46,200
 It doesn't execute because the preconditions aren't met because you're in the wrong spot

669
00:46:46,200 --> 00:46:47,280
 in the block, right?

670
00:46:47,280 --> 00:46:51,720
 Like the swaps already happened, your transaction executes, something like your condition has

671
00:46:51,720 --> 00:46:52,720
 changed.

672
00:46:52,720 --> 00:46:58,520
 You still spend some gas or like something putting the transaction into the chain.

673
00:46:58,520 --> 00:47:04,760
 But it's not, this is not productive economic activity for the most part.

674
00:47:04,760 --> 00:47:11,320
 And so, you know, they basically found that like the, because like you could do a hundred

675
00:47:11,320 --> 00:47:17,560
 of these, and if on the hundred and first like dumb idiot order, you like win it big,

676
00:47:17,560 --> 00:47:19,360
 it becomes positive EV for you.

677
00:47:19,360 --> 00:47:24,140
 So basically like it's advantageous for you to spam the blockchain with these fizzling

678
00:47:24,140 --> 00:47:27,960
 orders in the off chance that one of them gets through.

679
00:47:27,960 --> 00:47:34,160
 And so like these blockchains are just like a glut of these like insane order structures

680
00:47:34,160 --> 00:47:38,880
 getting pushed into every block because it is, it is economically positive.

681
00:47:38,880 --> 00:47:39,880
 But as a result,

682
00:47:39,880 --> 00:47:45,560
 But also the main thing is that many of these are like inert, basically, like it, like the,

683
00:47:45,560 --> 00:47:48,440
 they attempted a transaction, it failed.

684
00:47:48,440 --> 00:47:53,600
 And the thrust of the transaction is that they spent some gas to like compensate the

685
00:47:53,600 --> 00:47:57,920
 blockchain for doing the compute to like check that their transaction failed.

686
00:47:57,920 --> 00:48:00,120
 But there's like, they're not transacting with anyone else.

687
00:48:00,120 --> 00:48:01,120
 Exactly.

688
00:48:01,120 --> 00:48:02,120
 They're doing literally nothing.

689
00:48:02,120 --> 00:48:08,920
 They're just like clogging up the block space and like these L2s are full of it.

690
00:48:08,920 --> 00:48:13,100
 And you know, flashbots proposed a bunch of solutions.

691
00:48:13,100 --> 00:48:17,380
 One of the solutions that came out of it was EIP 1559, which basically is a progressive

692
00:48:17,380 --> 00:48:19,920
 tax on gas.

693
00:48:19,920 --> 00:48:25,600
 So the more, you know, clogged the chain is the more you have to pay.

694
00:48:25,600 --> 00:48:30,880
 And this is to obviously disincentivize these bots from, or at least making them find like

695
00:48:30,880 --> 00:48:34,800
 the price equilibrium that they're willing to pay to get that transaction in 1559 was

696
00:48:34,800 --> 00:48:37,400
